1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
I'm Terry Gerard and I'm Scoutmaster with Troop44 in Consville, Oklahoma.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:11,360
This edition of the Scoutmaster podcast is sponsored by backers like me.

3
00:00:11,360 --> 00:00:19,800
And now for you, Scoutmaster,

4
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:24,680
I have here in front of me another report of a discovery made by the dedicated research

5
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:30,240
Scouters at the Large Baden Collider out in Heisenberg, Arizona,

6
00:00:31,680 --> 00:00:33,680
Arizona. huh,

7
00:00:34,280 --> 00:00:39,160
I thought I thought Heisenberg was in Germany, but I'm uncertain about that.

8
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:44,480
Well, look anyway, the Large Baden Collider accelerates camping gear

9
00:00:44,480 --> 00:00:48,280
to its terminal velocity and things collide, apparently,

10
00:00:48,280 --> 00:00:53,800
And they discover interesting properties of different bits of camping gear.

11
00:00:54,240 --> 00:00:58,360
and the discovery that this report announces has to do with water filters,

12
00:00:58,360 --> 00:01:01,200
And it's called the water filter temporal distortion.

13
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:07,200
What they've discovered is that the stated operational time required

14
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:13,480
to filter a quart of water, expressed in minutes, will actually take

15
00:01:13,480 --> 00:01:15,680
an equal number of hours.

16
00:01:17,280 --> 00:01:21,800
So I guess if you've ever filtered water with a water filter,

17
00:01:21,800 --> 00:01:25,200
you know what I'm talking about.

18
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:27,759
Hey, this is podcast number 284..

19
00:01:52,560 --> 00:01:55,759
Well, welcome back to the Scoutmaster podcast.

20
00:01:55,759 --> 00:01:59,720
This is Clark Green, back to our regular good old format.

21
00:01:59,720 --> 00:02:02,160
We had a vacation day last week

22
00:02:02,160 --> 00:02:05,479
And but here we are, we're back again.

23
00:02:05,880 --> 00:02:08,039
Fred Glover is an OH chapter advisor.

24
00:02:08,039 --> 00:02:11,400
He wrote in to say thanks for your continued support of the scouting movement.

25
00:02:11,680 --> 00:02:13,840
I've only been listening to the podcast for a couple of years,

26
00:02:13,840 --> 00:02:17,320
but it always seemed that you were on a subject or issue

27
00:02:17,320 --> 00:02:19,079
I was encountering at the time.

28
00:02:19,079 --> 00:02:19,920
Thanks, Fred,

29
00:02:19,920 --> 00:02:21,280
I really appreciate that.

30
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:25,960
And thanks for being a backer to. Fred did mention that in his message to me.

31
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:29,200
Also, this message from Joe Prespari,

32
00:02:29,480 --> 00:02:32,480
who is the Cubmaster of Pac-23 in Burlington, Connecticut.

33
00:02:32,760 --> 00:02:33,360
Hi, Clark,

34
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:35,640
Well, you've inspired me to stay in Cub scouting next year

35
00:02:35,640 --> 00:02:38,040
and allow my son to move up into Boy Scouts.

36
00:02:38,320 --> 00:02:43,040
I really see a need in my town for seasoned leaders to remain in Cub Scouts

37
00:02:43,040 --> 00:02:45,600
and welcome the next generation of boys into the program.

38
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:51,320
It's my hope I can sway some more experienced tenured leaders to do the same.

39
00:02:51,880 --> 00:02:54,640
Otherwise we're going to lose all that experience at the Cub level.

40
00:02:54,640 --> 00:02:55,640
Thanks for the good work.

41
00:02:55,640 --> 00:02:57,840
Well, Joe, thank you.

42
00:02:57,840 --> 00:03:02,000
I'm glad that I had some small part in you're deciding to stay on with

43
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:04,320
Cubs and to stay active there.

44
00:03:04,519 --> 00:03:06,720
This is something we've talked about in the past, right

45
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:10,760
That that a lot of times we just expect to lose our most experienced

46
00:03:10,760 --> 00:03:15,160
Cub leaders when their own sons move up into a Boy Scout troop.

47
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:17,280
But Joe's taken a little bit of a different tack.

48
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:21,000
I think that's both noble and really, really necessary.

49
00:03:21,079 --> 00:03:23,400
So once again, thanks for being in touch, Joe.

50
00:03:23,760 --> 00:03:28,640
Hey, last week we were on with live chats and these happen usually

51
00:03:28,640 --> 00:03:32,519
on Tuesday and Wednesday mornings, Eastern time, starting about eight o'clock.

52
00:03:32,519 --> 00:03:37,240
We go to about 11 o'clock. keep an eye on my Facebook feed and my Twitter feed

53
00:03:37,320 --> 00:03:41,280
and come and join us at scoutmastercgcom for a live chat.

54
00:03:41,280 --> 00:03:43,760
And I wanted to say thank you to Scott Jones,

55
00:03:44,480 --> 00:03:49,440
who's a scout master for Troop 911 in San Antonio, Texas, and Jeremy Snyder,

56
00:03:49,720 --> 00:03:54,399
who's an assistant scout master with Troop 175 in North Ridgeville, Ohio.

57
00:03:54,840 --> 00:03:57,720
Scott and Jeremy both signed on for the first time

58
00:03:58,079 --> 00:04:02,239
and joined the rest of the frequent flyers that show up when we have a live chat.

59
00:04:02,359 --> 00:04:03,920
Thanks for spending a little time with me.

60
00:04:03,920 --> 00:04:05,440
I appreciate it.

61
00:04:05,440 --> 00:04:09,840
Let me take time to ask you a favor, because I- because I do need your help.

62
00:04:10,080 --> 00:04:15,600
I started blogging and podcasting back in 2005 and I really didn't plan

63
00:04:15,920 --> 00:04:18,240
on what happened in the next 10 years.

64
00:04:18,240 --> 00:04:21,480
It just kind of grew and I hear from a lot of folks every week

65
00:04:21,600 --> 00:04:26,440
and it's really heartening to know I'm able to lend them a hand in their work as scouts.

66
00:04:27,080 --> 00:04:32,120
So what I'm asking for is if you're finding the podcast and the blog posts

67
00:04:32,120 --> 00:04:36,560
and all the other things that we create at scoutmastercgcom useful to you.

68
00:04:36,840 --> 00:04:41,480
you can return the favor by becoming a backer, and it's pretty easy to do.

69
00:04:41,480 --> 00:04:45,640
Go to scoutmastercgcom, click the support link at the top of the page

70
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:49,880
and you'll find a number of options on making a one-time kind of voluntary

71
00:04:49,880 --> 00:04:52,600
subscription payment to keep things up and running.

72
00:04:53,080 --> 00:04:56,080
And I want to take a moment to personally thank Owen Wilson

73
00:04:56,360 --> 00:05:01,160
and Jamie Barnes, who became backers since the last podcast.

74
00:05:01,520 --> 00:05:04,000
Once again, go to scoutmastercgcom,

75
00:05:04,560 --> 00:05:07,880
Go to the support link at the top of the page, become a backer this week

76
00:05:07,880 --> 00:05:11,080
and I'll be sure to thank you on our next podcast.

77
00:05:11,360 --> 00:05:16,440
Now. last week I posted my thoughts about offering alternative wording

78
00:05:16,440 --> 00:05:21,320
to the Scout Oath that would be more in line with the BSA's approval

79
00:05:21,320 --> 00:05:28,000
of non-theist religious groups for participation in the Religious Emblems Program.

80
00:05:28,320 --> 00:05:32,520
I'm not going to try and explain everything that was in that post

81
00:05:32,719 --> 00:05:33,960
here. on the podcast,

82
00:05:34,039 --> 00:05:38,400
If you go to scoutmastercgcom, look for the post that's titled

83
00:05:38,680 --> 00:05:43,880
A Scout's Duty to God, and I'll link to it in the post that contains this podcast too.

84
00:05:44,799 --> 00:05:47,560
Now, whenever I mention debatable issues like this,

85
00:05:47,560 --> 00:05:51,240
I expect a strong response from both sides of the debate,

86
00:05:51,400 --> 00:05:54,840
And I'm not real comfortable talking about debatable issues most of the time,

87
00:05:55,240 --> 00:05:57,960
because it can be divisive, right?

88
00:05:58,799 --> 00:06:03,159
Discussing things like this with people is always a little tricky, isn't it?

89
00:06:03,479 --> 00:06:07,560
And discussing it online is even more complicated,

90
00:06:07,840 --> 00:06:12,680
But I wanted to say how much I appreciate everyone who took the time

91
00:06:12,680 --> 00:06:18,120
to join in the conversation and how much I appreciate the tone

92
00:06:18,560 --> 00:06:22,200
of almost everybody who joined in.

93
00:06:22,599 --> 00:06:26,120
You know, some people get a little strident about things

94
00:06:26,320 --> 00:06:29,600
in an inappropriate manner online, and that's going to happen,

95
00:06:29,960 --> 00:06:33,200
But when we're talking about scouts, for the most part, people you know

96
00:06:33,560 --> 00:06:36,440
are very respectful of each other's opinions.

97
00:06:36,840 --> 00:06:40,880
What that really gets me thinking about, though, is whenever

98
00:06:41,280 --> 00:06:43,840
we end up talking about these issues- right-

99
00:06:43,840 --> 00:06:46,640
And these are usually issues affecting membership

100
00:06:46,640 --> 00:06:51,560
and how we interpret and present the principles that scouting is based on-

101
00:06:51,920 --> 00:06:55,280
You have to examine what you're doing as an organization.

102
00:06:55,640 --> 00:06:58,880
I've spent the whole of my adult life being a scouter

103
00:06:59,200 --> 00:07:02,200
and being associated with the Boy Scouts of America,

104
00:07:02,560 --> 00:07:06,760
And you know the good old BSA has been through some difficult times

105
00:07:06,760 --> 00:07:11,400
in its century of existence, but perhaps none are more difficult

106
00:07:11,400 --> 00:07:13,360
than the times that we're in now,

107
00:07:13,360 --> 00:07:17,320
And when I think about organizations and changes and things like that,

108
00:07:17,720 --> 00:07:23,160
I think I think a useful allegory is that organizations

109
00:07:23,160 --> 00:07:25,280
are kind of like ships on the ocean.

110
00:07:25,680 --> 00:07:28,200
Now, if you're going to build a ship that carries a lot of people,

111
00:07:28,480 --> 00:07:31,280
you want to build something that's stable and safe,

112
00:07:31,520 --> 00:07:35,920
that reacts predictably to the winds and waves and currents and things,

113
00:07:36,680 --> 00:07:39,880
And so when you build an organization as big as the BSA,

114
00:07:40,680 --> 00:07:42,120
it's got to be stable.

115
00:07:42,120 --> 00:07:46,960
But to get that stability sometimes you have to be kind of impenetrable

116
00:07:46,960 --> 00:07:49,640
and inflexible like a ship, right?

117
00:07:49,840 --> 00:07:51,440
I mean, if you're building a big ship,

118
00:07:51,480 --> 00:07:53,520
you don't want to build the whole out of cellophane,

119
00:07:53,840 --> 00:07:56,760
You want to build it out of something pretty impenetrable

120
00:07:56,760 --> 00:07:58,680
and inflexible like steel, right?

121
00:07:59,560 --> 00:08:01,360
And we build organizations that way.

122
00:08:01,360 --> 00:08:03,760
We build them to be very stable,

123
00:08:04,080 --> 00:08:07,760
And that makes things kind of difficult. when it comes to evolution and change.

124
00:08:08,200 --> 00:08:12,440
The best ship is still going to have a lot of drag associated with it,

125
00:08:12,440 --> 00:08:16,640
And when it's a really big ship, it's going to take a lot of time to turn.

126
00:08:17,080 --> 00:08:20,560
And so, if you follow my allegory, the ocean that we're floating on

127
00:08:20,600 --> 00:08:23,680
is the culture, and culture is pretty wide and deep

128
00:08:23,680 --> 00:08:28,080
And it's more fluid and changeable than the organizational ships

129
00:08:28,080 --> 00:08:29,800
that we're traveling in.

130
00:08:29,800 --> 00:08:35,120
Big organizations like the BSA embrace cultural change pretty slowly,

131
00:08:35,520 --> 00:08:39,120
But to add to the complexity of the whole thing,

132
00:08:39,760 --> 00:08:44,400
our individual members and our young people they embrace change pretty quickly.

133
00:08:44,960 --> 00:08:46,800
Yeah, you ever noticed something about scouts?

134
00:08:46,839 --> 00:08:51,040
Scouts have this kind of unsettling ability

135
00:08:51,400 --> 00:08:55,240
to take the values and ethics that scouting has developed in them

136
00:08:55,680 --> 00:09:00,520
and to apply them without much regard to what the broader organization has to say.

137
00:09:00,880 --> 00:09:04,400
Scouts take things literally. and a really good example of that:

138
00:09:04,640 --> 00:09:07,839
Go work on a camp staff with a lot of young people sometime

139
00:09:07,959 --> 00:09:10,839
and you'll you'll understand exactly what what I mean.

140
00:09:11,280 --> 00:09:15,760
They understand the values and the ethics of scouting

141
00:09:16,120 --> 00:09:19,439
And sometimes they get crosswise to organizational imperatives.

142
00:09:19,439 --> 00:09:22,960
They come to a different conclusion about exactly how these things

143
00:09:22,960 --> 00:09:24,280
ought to be carried out.

144
00:09:24,280 --> 00:09:28,240
And I'll tell you something: anything like the BSA

145
00:09:28,240 --> 00:09:31,640
is going to find itself on the wrong side of history from time to time.

146
00:09:31,920 --> 00:09:36,160
Big national organizations are reluctant to face cultural changes,

147
00:09:36,439 --> 00:09:39,439
especially when championing a set of values and ethics

148
00:09:39,439 --> 00:09:42,720
that we say are unchanging and unalterable.

149
00:09:42,880 --> 00:09:45,760
But if you look at the history of the scouting movement

150
00:09:45,760 --> 00:09:49,120
and scouting organizations, there's been a lot of changes

151
00:09:49,120 --> 00:09:53,280
about how they interpret and express the values that we hold.

152
00:09:53,640 --> 00:09:56,160
We find new meanings, our world grows bigger,

153
00:09:56,160 --> 00:09:59,560
we become more diverse, and that diversity makes us stronger.

154
00:09:59,600 --> 00:10:03,080
You can look at diversity as being something that dilutes you,

155
00:10:03,280 --> 00:10:07,440
But I don't agree. OK. I think diversity makes us very strong.

156
00:10:07,880 --> 00:10:12,080
Now I'm 55 years old and if you're a part of my generation or older,

157
00:10:12,080 --> 00:10:13,720
I've got something to share with you,

158
00:10:13,720 --> 00:10:15,760
And if you're younger, you can keep listening,

159
00:10:15,760 --> 00:10:18,280
That's OK. But think about this:

160
00:10:19,160 --> 00:10:24,720
Our grandparents notice things that they didn't like in our parents' generation,

161
00:10:25,680 --> 00:10:28,760
And our parents noticed the same types of things

162
00:10:28,760 --> 00:10:32,040
they didn't like about our generation,

163
00:10:32,760 --> 00:10:37,320
And now it's basically our turn to be parents and grandparents

164
00:10:37,320 --> 00:10:41,560
and notice in our children's and our grandchildren's generation

165
00:10:41,800 --> 00:10:44,040
Things that we don't particularly approve of

166
00:10:44,040 --> 00:10:49,360
or things that seem to have changed, things that look scary to us.

167
00:10:49,840 --> 00:10:53,280
What you and I are noticing is basically the same thing

168
00:10:53,280 --> 00:10:56,160
that our parents and our grandparents were noticing.

169
00:10:56,520 --> 00:10:59,960
It's not so much things changing around us

170
00:11:00,280 --> 00:11:05,440
as our own perspective changing as we are getting a little older.

171
00:11:06,680 --> 00:11:08,800
Yeah, things changed around us.

172
00:11:08,840 --> 00:11:11,440
I understand that, but I think we older people

173
00:11:11,440 --> 00:11:16,480
fall into this comfortable trap of looking at anything that's changing

174
00:11:16,480 --> 00:11:19,240
and moaning and groaning about how things are different now,

175
00:11:19,720 --> 00:11:22,720
because we're a little bit afraid of what comes next. for us,

176
00:11:23,280 --> 00:11:26,720
It's harder to accept changes and to look at things

177
00:11:27,040 --> 00:11:29,400
from new perspectives the older you get-

178
00:11:29,960 --> 00:11:33,360
And also men. we need to defer to the youthful energy

179
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:37,480
and initiative of young people, of our scouts and younger scouts.

180
00:11:37,480 --> 00:11:40,160
We need to listen because there's a lot of wisdom

181
00:11:40,160 --> 00:11:43,560
in what they're having to say and they can help us navigate

182
00:11:44,040 --> 00:11:47,160
what's changing around us, because I'm afraid otherwise

183
00:11:47,560 --> 00:11:49,560
we'll have an organization with a few scouts

184
00:11:49,560 --> 00:11:51,920
and a lot of old geezers like me. right,

185
00:11:52,760 --> 00:11:55,040
And I got to say it's the old geezers

186
00:11:55,040 --> 00:11:57,840
that kind of run the show. as far as the PSA is concerned,

187
00:11:57,840 --> 00:12:00,040
If you go to a meeting, take a look around,

188
00:12:00,040 --> 00:12:01,960
You see the age, gender and race

189
00:12:01,960 --> 00:12:05,840
of the people who are making decisions is pretty much, you know,

190
00:12:05,840 --> 00:12:07,240
pretty much the same.

191
00:12:07,280 --> 00:12:08,800
And when we old geezers sit around

192
00:12:08,800 --> 00:12:11,000
we're not too sure what's going wrong.

193
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,280
We think we just have to market ourselves better

194
00:12:13,280 --> 00:12:17,160
or repackage our message or add more programs

195
00:12:17,160 --> 00:12:21,560
that 50 plus year old men think boys would be interested in.

196
00:12:22,400 --> 00:12:25,400
But what scouts do is not the problem.

197
00:12:25,400 --> 00:12:28,440
That program is still relevant, exciting and engaging.

198
00:12:28,840 --> 00:12:30,760
We don't have to adorn it with bobbles

199
00:12:30,760 --> 00:12:32,560
or repackage it in shiny paper.

200
00:12:32,560 --> 00:12:36,160
We've tried that many, many times before and it never works.

201
00:12:36,480 --> 00:12:38,640
Those aren't the kind of changes we need.

202
00:12:39,160 --> 00:12:40,920
I'm saying that most of the people

203
00:12:40,920 --> 00:12:43,920
who are making the decisions in the PSA

204
00:12:43,920 --> 00:12:46,079
are kind of old geezers like me,

205
00:12:46,079 --> 00:12:48,760
And I don't think they're bad people, far from it.

206
00:12:48,760 --> 00:12:51,199
I know that they're people of goodwill

207
00:12:51,199 --> 00:12:54,160
as concerned with and engaged in scouting as anybody.

208
00:12:54,600 --> 00:12:57,199
I do think, though, that they're reluctant

209
00:12:57,199 --> 00:12:59,240
to make any big changes, and I can't blame them,

210
00:12:59,240 --> 00:13:02,040
because change is scary and uncertain

211
00:13:02,400 --> 00:13:04,839
And it can be tremendously difficult,

212
00:13:04,840 --> 00:13:07,720
It can be kind of exhausting and it can be divisive,

213
00:13:08,520 --> 00:13:11,280
And change can be seen as an admission

214
00:13:11,720 --> 00:13:14,440
that you were doing things wrong, and that's humbling,

215
00:13:15,040 --> 00:13:18,480
And so it becomes a very complex question, doesn't it?

216
00:13:19,280 --> 00:13:21,040
But I have to say I've watched the rest

217
00:13:21,040 --> 00:13:23,560
of the world scouting movement confront

218
00:13:24,080 --> 00:13:27,480
the changes the society makes over the past few decades

219
00:13:27,880 --> 00:13:30,760
and do the hard work required to serve

220
00:13:30,800 --> 00:13:32,960
as many youth as possible

221
00:13:33,400 --> 00:13:36,080
with the really wonderful program

222
00:13:36,080 --> 00:13:39,520
and the vast potential that scouting promises.

223
00:13:39,960 --> 00:13:43,160
And I don't think anybody from any of the different

224
00:13:43,160 --> 00:13:45,040
organizations in the world of scouting

225
00:13:45,280 --> 00:13:47,560
would tell you that change was easy, because it isn't.

226
00:13:47,560 --> 00:13:51,560
But even so, these organizations have stabilized

227
00:13:51,560 --> 00:13:54,360
and even grown over the past 15 years,

228
00:13:54,680 --> 00:13:57,600
as our beloved, arthritic old PSA

229
00:13:57,960 --> 00:14:02,040
has shrunk by one third of its youth members,

230
00:14:02,360 --> 00:14:05,280
That's a million young people,

231
00:14:05,480 --> 00:14:07,920
If you sit and talk about it and you think about it. of course

232
00:14:08,319 --> 00:14:11,079
there's a number of different reasons that membership is down,

233
00:14:11,400 --> 00:14:14,040
But I think what we have to ask ourselves seriously

234
00:14:14,640 --> 00:14:17,839
is what our organization will look like five years from now

235
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:20,920
If we were to not make any changes at all,

236
00:14:20,920 --> 00:14:24,680
not to ask ourselves questions and innovate our programs

237
00:14:24,680 --> 00:14:28,360
and our approaches, because I think that future looks pretty grim.

238
00:14:29,160 --> 00:14:31,160
But if we listen to our young people,

239
00:14:31,280 --> 00:14:34,520
we'll find a lot of energy and inspiration in what they have to say

240
00:14:34,959 --> 00:14:40,480
And if we understand our aim is to give as many young people

241
00:14:40,480 --> 00:14:44,560
as possible the advantage of having the wonderful opportunities

242
00:14:44,560 --> 00:14:46,880
that scouting offers, we're going to make some changes

243
00:14:46,959 --> 00:14:48,880
and we're going to start to see things look up.

244
00:14:48,880 --> 00:14:50,000
So there we go.

245
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:52,880
I'll get down off my soapbox now and we'll move on,

246
00:14:53,160 --> 00:14:54,719
And in the remainder of this podcast

247
00:14:54,719 --> 00:14:56,280
I've got some email questions to answer.

248
00:14:56,280 --> 00:14:58,040
So let's get started. shall we

249
00:15:32,120 --> 00:15:34,120
Send it by name

250
00:15:35,600 --> 00:15:37,120
Email? that is, folks.

251
00:15:37,920 --> 00:15:40,760
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

252
00:15:50,160 --> 00:15:53,959
Brittany Henson is from PAC 691 in Longwood, Florida,

253
00:15:54,240 --> 00:15:55,839
And she wrote to say: I'm a scout mom.

254
00:15:55,839 --> 00:15:59,560
I've recently taken over the leadership of my son,

255
00:16:00,440 --> 00:16:02,920
My son's- Weeblows 1 den

256
00:16:03,160 --> 00:16:07,840
And I've been told I need to run the den like a Boy Scout patrol.

257
00:16:08,320 --> 00:16:11,400
I like the idea, but I'm a little confused.

258
00:16:11,400 --> 00:16:13,600
Do you have any pointers on how I can do this

259
00:16:13,600 --> 00:16:16,280
And what are the main points of a Boy Scout patrol?

260
00:16:17,560 --> 00:16:18,920
How strict is the group size?

261
00:16:18,920 --> 00:16:21,800
I only have four Weeblows right now.

262
00:16:22,040 --> 00:16:24,680
I do have all the information on Weeblows requirements

263
00:16:24,680 --> 00:16:25,960
and the new program.

264
00:16:25,960 --> 00:16:28,280
I guess what I don't entirely understand

265
00:16:28,280 --> 00:16:30,079
is the purpose of a patrol

266
00:16:30,400 --> 00:16:32,720
Other than the boys and I meeting once a week.

267
00:16:32,720 --> 00:16:34,280
are there other considerations?

268
00:16:35,120 --> 00:16:37,079
Well, Brittany, that's an excellent question,

269
00:16:37,079 --> 00:16:39,680
And as for what scout patrols do

270
00:16:39,680 --> 00:16:41,319
and how you incorporate it into Weeblows,

271
00:16:41,319 --> 00:16:43,160
it's not all that difficult or mysterious

272
00:16:43,160 --> 00:16:44,400
to tell you the truth.

273
00:16:44,400 --> 00:16:47,040
So don't let the idea of a patrol confuse you,

274
00:16:47,040 --> 00:16:50,319
because it's actually pretty much the way things

275
00:16:50,319 --> 00:16:54,040
work, naturally with scouts and with boys. to begin with,

276
00:16:54,319 --> 00:16:57,400
A patrol is just really a small group of scouts

277
00:16:57,400 --> 00:17:00,360
who work together under their own leadership.

278
00:17:00,920 --> 00:17:03,360
Now in Weeblows, the boys are of an age

279
00:17:03,360 --> 00:17:05,079
where they can begin to take on

280
00:17:05,079 --> 00:17:07,319
more and more responsibility for themselves,

281
00:17:07,319 --> 00:17:09,000
And we want to encourage that.

282
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:11,119
As a den leader, you'll want to explain

283
00:17:11,119 --> 00:17:12,440
that they're getting a little older.

284
00:17:12,440 --> 00:17:13,800
It's time for them to start learning

285
00:17:13,800 --> 00:17:15,720
how to work together as a patrol

286
00:17:15,720 --> 00:17:17,520
and how to lead each other

287
00:17:17,520 --> 00:17:19,680
through the things that they do.

288
00:17:20,240 --> 00:17:22,280
So they can do things like choose a patrol name

289
00:17:22,280 --> 00:17:23,720
and elect a patrol leader,

290
00:17:23,720 --> 00:17:27,000
and the patrol leader can start helping you lead the den.

291
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,520
I think it'd be a good idea to give all the Weeblows

292
00:17:29,520 --> 00:17:31,920
the chance to be patrol leader,

293
00:17:31,920 --> 00:17:33,760
So you may want to have a talk with them

294
00:17:33,760 --> 00:17:35,880
about exactly how you're going to make that happen,

295
00:17:35,880 --> 00:17:37,520
And talking to them is something

296
00:17:37,520 --> 00:17:39,280
that I really encourage you to do.

297
00:17:39,280 --> 00:17:41,400
Look at your work as a den leader

298
00:17:41,400 --> 00:17:45,240
as helping the Weeblows organize themselves

299
00:17:45,240 --> 00:17:46,360
and make things happen,

300
00:17:46,360 --> 00:17:48,360
rather than thinking you have to be the expert

301
00:17:48,360 --> 00:17:49,640
and know all the answers.

302
00:17:49,640 --> 00:17:52,480
I'm a big advocate of talking to our scouts

303
00:17:52,480 --> 00:17:54,600
about how they'd like to make things happen.

304
00:17:54,600 --> 00:17:56,200
You don't have to know at all

305
00:17:56,280 --> 00:17:57,520
or be an expert.

306
00:17:57,520 --> 00:17:59,600
You can learn right along with them.

307
00:17:59,600 --> 00:18:03,120
If you take the materials that explain the program,

308
00:18:03,120 --> 00:18:04,920
if you take the Weeblows handbook

309
00:18:04,920 --> 00:18:06,520
and you start discussing with them

310
00:18:06,520 --> 00:18:07,920
how to make things happen

311
00:18:07,920 --> 00:18:10,040
and you use those resources

312
00:18:10,040 --> 00:18:12,360
to define the way you're going to do it,

313
00:18:12,360 --> 00:18:14,840
you'll be surprised at how much they can actually do

314
00:18:14,840 --> 00:18:16,840
and how excited they are

315
00:18:16,840 --> 00:18:20,000
when they're actually having something to do

316
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:22,120
with leading themselves.

317
00:18:22,120 --> 00:18:25,080
I heard from William Castler in Wasilla, Alaska,

318
00:18:25,080 --> 00:18:26,679
and he said. 22 years ago

319
00:18:26,679 --> 00:18:28,120
my boys came home

320
00:18:28,120 --> 00:18:30,120
and they asked me if they could join scouting.

321
00:18:30,120 --> 00:18:30,960
I said okay

322
00:18:30,960 --> 00:18:33,560
and I found myself as the PAC committee chairman.

323
00:18:33,560 --> 00:18:35,480
So I've been a den leader, assistant scout,

324
00:18:35,480 --> 00:18:36,480
master, unit commissioner,

325
00:18:36,480 --> 00:18:39,840
merit badge counselor and more besides,

326
00:18:39,840 --> 00:18:41,080
And when I first got started

327
00:18:41,080 --> 00:18:43,280
I found it difficult to find good information,

328
00:18:43,280 --> 00:18:45,679
especially when working with parents and other adults.

329
00:18:45,679 --> 00:18:46,520
Several years ago

330
00:18:46,520 --> 00:18:48,199
I started to put out a weekly newsletter

331
00:18:48,199 --> 00:18:50,439
and I've included your excellent posts

332
00:18:50,439 --> 00:18:53,240
and your material in my newsletter as often as I could,

333
00:18:53,240 --> 00:18:55,480
but I'm in the process of changing the format

334
00:18:55,480 --> 00:18:56,720
to a webpage

335
00:18:56,720 --> 00:18:59,160
and I'm writing to ask if you'd had any objections

336
00:18:59,160 --> 00:19:00,480
if I took your posts

337
00:19:00,480 --> 00:19:03,240
and used them on this new site.

338
00:19:03,240 --> 00:19:04,600
Before I answer, William,

339
00:19:04,600 --> 00:19:06,240
I also heard from Pierre,

340
00:19:06,240 --> 00:19:07,960
who said I'm working on a little camp book

341
00:19:07,960 --> 00:19:09,280
for my scouts

342
00:19:09,280 --> 00:19:11,600
with useful tips and things for them to use.

343
00:19:11,600 --> 00:19:13,040
I'd like to use some of the material

344
00:19:13,040 --> 00:19:13,880
that you've produced

345
00:19:13,880 --> 00:19:15,480
and translated into French,

346
00:19:15,480 --> 00:19:17,320
and I can send the finished book

347
00:19:17,320 --> 00:19:18,880
and all the translated documents

348
00:19:18,880 --> 00:19:20,240
that you add to your website.

349
00:19:20,240 --> 00:19:22,360
I wanted to find out if that's okay with you,

350
00:19:22,360 --> 00:19:24,480
and that's from Pierre Courdiere,

351
00:19:24,480 --> 00:19:26,520
who is with the FSS,

352
00:19:26,520 --> 00:19:30,360
the Fraternal Salut Scouts in France.

353
00:19:30,360 --> 00:19:32,280
So William and Pierre

354
00:19:32,280 --> 00:19:33,560
and everybody else is listening.

355
00:19:33,560 --> 00:19:34,840
Anything that I've made,

356
00:19:34,840 --> 00:19:36,520
anything I've written or produced,

357
00:19:36,520 --> 00:19:38,280
the infographics and all that stuff

358
00:19:38,280 --> 00:19:40,400
is freely offered to support scouting,

359
00:19:40,400 --> 00:19:41,840
no matter where you are.

360
00:19:41,840 --> 00:19:43,560
The only thing that I ask

361
00:19:43,560 --> 00:19:45,520
is that if you use anything

362
00:19:45,520 --> 00:19:47,040
that I've created in your materials,

363
00:19:47,040 --> 00:19:50,120
all you do is let people know where you found it,

364
00:19:50,120 --> 00:19:52,080
Give them a link back to the website

365
00:19:52,080 --> 00:19:55,159
or to say that you found it at scoutmastercgcom.

366
00:19:55,159 --> 00:19:57,159
So if you're working on a project

367
00:19:57,159 --> 00:19:59,000
and you want to incorporate some of the material

368
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:00,399
or some of the writing that I've done,

369
00:20:00,399 --> 00:20:03,760
please take this as a blanket approval to do so,

370
00:20:03,760 --> 00:20:06,080
so long as you, like I said,

371
00:20:06,080 --> 00:20:08,040
give proper attribution to where you found it

372
00:20:08,040 --> 00:20:09,199
and who created it.

373
00:20:09,199 --> 00:20:10,800
Heard from Joel Irway.

374
00:20:10,800 --> 00:20:11,639
He said this:

375
00:20:11,639 --> 00:20:14,760
I'm an assistant scout master with Troop 637

376
00:20:14,760 --> 00:20:16,639
in Onsted, Michigan.

377
00:20:16,639 --> 00:20:18,240
As an assistant scout master,

378
00:20:18,240 --> 00:20:19,679
I'm not part of the committee,

379
00:20:19,679 --> 00:20:21,879
nor do I sit on boards of review

380
00:20:21,880 --> 00:20:24,120
and, in an effort to keep the Troop youth led,

381
00:20:24,120 --> 00:20:26,520
I'm really not supposed to have an influence

382
00:20:26,520 --> 00:20:28,880
on patrol leaders council meetings.

383
00:20:28,880 --> 00:20:32,280
So with all the stuff I am not supposed to do,

384
00:20:32,280 --> 00:20:34,960
I'm a little lost as to what I should be doing.

385
00:20:34,960 --> 00:20:37,440
I asked another assistant scout master from our Troop

386
00:20:37,440 --> 00:20:39,640
and he didn't have an answer for me either.

387
00:20:39,640 --> 00:20:42,360
We both felt like this was a good question for you

388
00:20:42,360 --> 00:20:43,920
and that it's pretty likely

389
00:20:43,920 --> 00:20:45,720
we're not the only assistant scout masters

390
00:20:45,720 --> 00:20:46,840
who are a little puzzled.

391
00:20:46,840 --> 00:20:48,840
Well, Joel, that is an excellent question.

392
00:20:48,840 --> 00:20:50,440
You're puzzled because, unfortunately,

393
00:20:50,440 --> 00:20:52,000
the way the vast majority of Troops

394
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:54,880
handle the assistant scout master position,

395
00:20:54,880 --> 00:20:55,720
I don't know,

396
00:20:55,720 --> 00:20:58,160
it just doesn't make a lot of sense to me. sometimes

397
00:20:58,160 --> 00:21:00,040
There are frankly-

398
00:21:00,040 --> 00:21:02,640
oh I'm gonna get myself in trouble by saying this,

399
00:21:02,640 --> 00:21:04,320
but listen, frankly,

400
00:21:04,320 --> 00:21:06,520
there are just too many adult volunteers

401
00:21:06,520 --> 00:21:08,640
who have little or no idea

402
00:21:08,640 --> 00:21:12,560
of what scouting is or their role in making it happen.

403
00:21:12,560 --> 00:21:13,760
Somewhere along the way,

404
00:21:13,760 --> 00:21:14,760
I think we got the idea

405
00:21:14,760 --> 00:21:17,840
that the more volunteers we have, the better,

406
00:21:17,840 --> 00:21:21,159
but this is not necessarily the case. okay,

407
00:21:21,159 --> 00:21:23,040
If you have a troop with 30 scouts

408
00:21:23,040 --> 00:21:25,120
and 12 assistant scout masters,

409
00:21:25,120 --> 00:21:27,679
what you really end up with is either

410
00:21:27,679 --> 00:21:30,360
the assistant scout masters kind of doing the things

411
00:21:30,360 --> 00:21:32,000
that the scouts are supposed to do,

412
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:34,199
or you have a parents club within the troop,

413
00:21:34,199 --> 00:21:36,639
and that can cause problems too,

414
00:21:36,639 --> 00:21:37,480
And it's great fun

415
00:21:37,480 --> 00:21:39,280
for all the people who are volunteering,

416
00:21:39,280 --> 00:21:41,840
but maybe not so much for the scouts who are involved.

417
00:21:41,840 --> 00:21:42,800
Now, to tell you the truth,

418
00:21:42,800 --> 00:21:44,760
there really shouldn't be very much

419
00:21:44,760 --> 00:21:46,360
for assistant scout masters-

420
00:21:46,360 --> 00:21:49,240
or the scout master for that matter- to do.

421
00:21:49,240 --> 00:21:52,280
I mean, when there's an engaged youth leadership

422
00:21:52,280 --> 00:21:54,120
running their program.

423
00:21:54,120 --> 00:21:55,320
you know, if you think about it,

424
00:21:55,320 --> 00:21:56,560
there's not a whole lot of use

425
00:21:56,560 --> 00:21:59,280
for more than two or three scouters on an outing.

426
00:21:59,280 --> 00:22:01,960
There's almost nothing for them to do at meetings

427
00:22:01,960 --> 00:22:04,879
that involve the program and the meeting directly.

428
00:22:04,879 --> 00:22:07,760
but I frequently see troops with 10 or more,

429
00:22:07,760 --> 00:22:10,360
and sometimes many more, assistant scout masters.

430
00:22:10,360 --> 00:22:12,360
I have to wonder what they actually do.

431
00:22:12,360 --> 00:22:15,480
Now, my ideal setup is a scout master

432
00:22:15,480 --> 00:22:17,400
with two assistant scout masters

433
00:22:17,400 --> 00:22:20,040
for any reasonably sized troop.

434
00:22:20,040 --> 00:22:21,800
One of the assistant scout masters

435
00:22:21,800 --> 00:22:24,720
is nominally the first assistant

436
00:22:24,720 --> 00:22:26,920
And he's the guy who's on deck to take over

437
00:22:26,920 --> 00:22:29,240
when the scout masters ready to hand things over.

438
00:22:29,240 --> 00:22:32,920
And ideally there'd be a date certain for that transition.

439
00:22:32,920 --> 00:22:35,520
We'd know, you know, that the current scout master

440
00:22:35,520 --> 00:22:37,640
is gonna serve for the next two or three years

441
00:22:37,640 --> 00:22:39,120
and we know the date

442
00:22:39,120 --> 00:22:40,640
where there's gonna be a transition.

443
00:22:40,640 --> 00:22:43,560
So our first assistant is getting prepared

444
00:22:43,560 --> 00:22:46,160
And there's a second assistant scout master

445
00:22:46,160 --> 00:22:49,320
who becomes the first assistant scout master

446
00:22:49,320 --> 00:22:51,120
when there's that transition.

447
00:22:51,120 --> 00:22:53,880
So at each transition of who's the scout master

448
00:22:53,880 --> 00:22:55,960
and assistant scout master, post comes open

449
00:22:55,960 --> 00:22:57,680
and the committee appoints someone

450
00:22:57,680 --> 00:23:00,680
who's fully committed to developing the skills required,

451
00:23:00,680 --> 00:23:01,680
And I think personally

452
00:23:01,680 --> 00:23:04,080
there should be some very high expectations

453
00:23:04,080 --> 00:23:06,920
of the adults who are chosen to be scout master

454
00:23:06,920 --> 00:23:08,440
and assistant scout master.

455
00:23:08,440 --> 00:23:11,240
Now, any responsible committee should be sure

456
00:23:11,240 --> 00:23:13,680
of who the next scout master will be,

457
00:23:13,680 --> 00:23:15,880
And ideally they'll have a couple of people

458
00:23:15,880 --> 00:23:17,720
ready to take the job on.

459
00:23:17,720 --> 00:23:19,440
Now, some years back

460
00:23:19,440 --> 00:23:21,960
we trimmed our assistant scout master list

461
00:23:21,960 --> 00:23:23,440
down to three or four

462
00:23:23,440 --> 00:23:24,960
And we asked all the other adults

463
00:23:24,960 --> 00:23:27,040
registered as assistant scout masters

464
00:23:27,040 --> 00:23:29,040
to change their registration

465
00:23:29,040 --> 00:23:30,280
to be members of the committee,

466
00:23:30,280 --> 00:23:31,720
And at that time

467
00:23:31,720 --> 00:23:33,400
we had a lot of assistant scout masters,

468
00:23:33,400 --> 00:23:34,520
probably close to 10..

469
00:23:34,520 --> 00:23:37,040
They were mostly doing committee work anyway

470
00:23:37,040 --> 00:23:38,879
And the committee was kind of thin.

471
00:23:38,879 --> 00:23:41,399
So changing the positions made a lot of sense

472
00:23:41,399 --> 00:23:42,240
And as a result

473
00:23:42,240 --> 00:23:44,480
we've had a pretty substantial advancement team

474
00:23:44,480 --> 00:23:46,320
of four or five people.

475
00:23:46,320 --> 00:23:47,720
So setting up boards of review

476
00:23:47,720 --> 00:23:49,760
and tracking advancement and things like that

477
00:23:49,760 --> 00:23:53,800
is pretty light lifting for a group that size.

478
00:23:53,800 --> 00:23:56,200
We have a gear guy- and presently that's me-

479
00:23:56,200 --> 00:23:57,600
who keeps an eye on all the gear

480
00:23:57,600 --> 00:23:59,200
and works with the quarter master

481
00:23:59,200 --> 00:24:01,399
and a number of other folks doing committee work

482
00:24:01,399 --> 00:24:03,120
of one kind or another.

483
00:24:03,120 --> 00:24:04,680
Now that's what works for me

484
00:24:04,680 --> 00:24:06,240
And that may help you

485
00:24:06,280 --> 00:24:08,800
and your troops sort out what happens next

486
00:24:08,800 --> 00:24:10,000
If you have a scout master

487
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:13,440
who doesn't know when they're going to be stepping down.

488
00:24:13,440 --> 00:24:14,960
I think it's an important discussion

489
00:24:14,960 --> 00:24:16,600
to have with the committee.

490
00:24:16,600 --> 00:24:18,840
I suggest most scout masters would serve

491
00:24:18,840 --> 00:24:21,760
for maybe a maximum of five years.

492
00:24:21,760 --> 00:24:24,000
I went on for 30.

493
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:25,400
And that's kind of an anomaly

494
00:24:25,400 --> 00:24:27,120
And I know there's a lot of long-tenured

495
00:24:27,120 --> 00:24:28,760
scout masters out there.

496
00:24:28,760 --> 00:24:30,040
But realistically

497
00:24:30,040 --> 00:24:31,800
I think five years,

498
00:24:31,800 --> 00:24:32,840
three to five years,

499
00:24:32,840 --> 00:24:36,000
is a pretty realistic commitment for most people.

500
00:24:36,440 --> 00:24:38,120
Then you have a date out there.

501
00:24:38,120 --> 00:24:40,760
It's not that we appoint a scout master

502
00:24:40,760 --> 00:24:44,520
and they serve on in perpetuity with no end in sight,

503
00:24:44,520 --> 00:24:46,800
because usually there's an end in sight

504
00:24:46,800 --> 00:24:48,960
And putting a date on that is gonna be helpful,

505
00:24:48,960 --> 00:24:51,320
because then the troop can appoint

506
00:24:51,320 --> 00:24:54,360
a first and second assistant scout master

507
00:24:54,360 --> 00:24:57,120
And then the assistant scout masters

508
00:24:57,120 --> 00:24:59,320
will have some real direction in what they're doing.

509
00:24:59,320 --> 00:25:01,800
So I think that any troops should decide

510
00:25:01,800 --> 00:25:05,000
how many assistant scout masters they actually need,

511
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:07,080
what expectations they should meet,

512
00:25:07,080 --> 00:25:09,280
and only appoint that many.

513
00:25:09,280 --> 00:25:11,320
